WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.095
Picture a café owner pressing Refund.

2
00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:11.503
The app takes 25.00 from her shop's balance, adds it to a customer's, and the screen says Refunded.

3
00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:13.821
A second later, the power fails.

4
00:00:13.821 --> 00:00:17.322
At that moment, neither balance had changed on disk.

5
00:00:17.322 --> 00:00:22.439
The database had only added about 200 bytes to the end of one file, the log.

6
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.447
So how does it rebuild both balances, and know what's already done?

7
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.207
A café's back room has the same problem.

8
00:00:29.207 --> 00:00:32.517
The stock counts sit on a whiteboard, quick to rub out.

9
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:37.572
But before anyone changes a number, they write the change in a notebook by the door.

10
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:41.080
If the whiteboard gets wiped, the notebook can rebuild it.

11
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:44.276
Nobody wants to replay a notebook from page one.

12
00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:47.850
So now and then, someone takes a photo of the whiteboard.

13
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.615
After a wipe, start from the last photo, and replay only the lines after it.

14
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:58.320
Map it: the whiteboard is the pages in memory, and the notebook is the log on disk.

15
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.294
The lab's model has four pages, one number each.

16
00:01:02.294 --> 00:01:05.141
At the last photo, page 1 held 1,000.

17
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:08.784
Transaction T1 moves 300 from page 1 to page 2.

18
00:01:08.784 --> 00:01:13.628
A transaction is a group of changes that must all happen, or none.

19
00:01:13.628 --> 00:01:18.473
It writes four records to the log: BEGIN, two updates, and COMMIT.

20
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:25.841
Each record's place in the log, counted in bytes, is its LSN, its log sequence number.

21
00:01:25.841 --> 00:01:29.477
COMMIT forces the log to disk up to LSN 116.

22
00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:33.196
The pages change in memory only, stamped 116.

23
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:40.952
Later, the background writer, a process that saves changed pages, writes page 1 to disk, LSN and all.

24
00:01:40.952 --> 00:01:43.281
Page 2 is still only in memory.

25
00:01:43.281 --> 00:01:45.909
That's fine: the log has all of T1.

26
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:53.179
Then T2 starts moving 50 from page 2 to page 3, and T3 sets page 4 to 42 and commits.

27
00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:59.371
T3's flush writes T2's records to disk too, because the log is one stream.

28
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.374
Then the power fails.

29
00:02:01.374 --> 00:02:02.665
Memory is gone.

30
00:02:02.665 --> 00:02:09.033
Left on disk: the log up to LSN 290, and pages that are partly up to date.

31
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:12.905
Page 1 lost its 300, and page 2 never got it.

32
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:14.880
Where should recovery start?

33
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.820
The log's first record would work, but that could take hours, and the log could never be thrown away.

34
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.158
That's the photo.

35
00:02:22.158 --> 00:02:30.198
A checkpoint writes every changed page to disk, forces it there, and then logs a redo point, where recovery may start.

36
00:02:30.198 --> 00:02:36.807
PostgreSQL, a free database program, takes one every 5 minutes, or after about a gigabyte of log.

37
00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:40.093
Recovery reads the log from the redo point.

38
00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:45.643
At T1's COMMIT, it redoes T1's changes, but only where a page is missing them.

39
00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:46.781
How can it tell?

40
00:02:46.967 --> 00:02:55.578
Page 1 on disk says LSN 116, so it already has every change up to 116: skip.

41
00:02:55.578 --> 00:03:01.017
Page 2 says LSN 0: apply the 300, and stamp 116.

42
00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:02.865
That stamp is the page LSN.

43
00:03:02.865 --> 00:03:07.644
On the whiteboard, it's the notebook line number written next to each count.

44
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.670
Next come T2's records.

45
00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.749
They're on disk, written by T3's commit.

46
00:03:12.749 --> 00:03:14.674
Does recovery apply them?

47
00:03:14.674 --> 00:03:15.905
Pause and guess.

48
00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:19.347
No.

49
00:03:19.347 --> 00:03:22.767
T2 has no COMMIT, so recovery drops its records.

50
00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:25.261
Here the whiteboard picture breaks.

51
00:03:25.261 --> 00:03:28.183
Every line in the café's notebook counts.

52
00:03:28.183 --> 00:03:32.031
A database log also holds changes that never finished.

53
00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:37.724
At T3's COMMIT, page 4 says LSN 0, below 290, so 42 goes in.

54
00:03:37.724 --> 00:03:41.842
After 290, the next record doesn't check out.

55
00:03:41.842 --> 00:03:44.221
That's the end of the log.

56
00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:45.772
Now the nasty case.

57
00:03:45.772 --> 00:03:49.071
The power fails again, halfway through recovery.

58
00:03:49.071 --> 00:03:51.202
What does the next recovery do?

59
00:03:51.202 --> 00:03:52.301
Pause and guess.

60
00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:58.439
Exactly the same work, with the same result.

61
00:03:58.439 --> 00:04:01.507
Doing it twice gives the same as doing it once.

62
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.139
That's called idempotent.

63
00:04:03.139 --> 00:04:07.839
Pull the plug on recovery as often as you like; it never holds a grudge.

64
00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:12.549
One limit of the lab: it never lets unfinished changes into pages.

65
00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:16.216
Real engines can, so they add a step that undoes them.

66
00:04:16.216 --> 00:04:22.871
PostgreSQL has none: a transaction with no commit record counts as cancelled, and stays invisible.

67
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.570
So, the refund was safe all along.

68
00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:36.032
About 200 bytes of log, forced at commit, held everything needed to rebuild both balances, and the page LSNs told recovery what was already there.

69
00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:44.411
The moral of the story: start at the last checkpoint, redo what a page is missing, skip what it has, and drop what never committed.

70
00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:46.095
One quick check.

71
00:04:46.095 --> 00:04:49.165
T2 also wanted to put 50 into page 3.

72
00:04:49.165 --> 00:04:52.319
After recovery, what does page 3 hold?

73
00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:53.647
Pause and think.

74
00:04:56.833 --> 00:04:57.213
Zero.

75
00:04:57.213 --> 00:05:03.823
T2 never committed, so its change was dropped, and page 3 is as the checkpoint left it.
